    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_pyf_211",
      "name": "defaults-and-scope-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Predict the output: defaults and scope",
      "description": "Read each snippet the way Python runs it: when are default values created, and which names are local?",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "What does this print?\n\ndef add_tag(tag, tags=[]):\n    tags.append(tag)\n    return tags\n\na = add_tag(\"gift\")\nb = add_tag(\"express\", [])\nc = add_tag(\"bulk\")\nprint(a, b, c)",
          "explanation": "The default list is created once, when def runs, and every call that leaves out tags gets that same list. The first call appends \"gift\" to it and returns it, so a IS the default list. The second call passes its own new list, so b is ['express'] and the default is untouched. The third call uses the default again and appends \"bulk\" - and returns the same list object that a already points to. a and c are two names for one list, so both print ['gift', 'bulk'].",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "['gift'] ['express'] ['bulk']",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "['gift'] ['express'] ['gift', 'bulk']",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "['gift', 'bulk'] ['express'] ['gift', 'bulk']",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "['gift', 'bulk'] ['gift', 'express'] ['gift', 'express', 'bulk']",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The module starts with:\n\nsynced = 0\nfailed = []\n\ndef a():\n    print(synced)\n\ndef b():\n    synced += 1\n\ndef c():\n    print(synced)\n    synced = 5\n\ndef d():\n    failed.append(\"LM-1001\")\n\ndef e():\n    global synced\n    synced += 1\n\nWhich of these functions raise UnboundLocalError when called? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "Python decides which names are local when it compiles the function: any name that is ASSIGNED anywhere in the body (=, +=, a for target...) is local in the whole body. `synced += 1` reads the local synced before it has a value, and in the print-then-assign version the later `synced = 5` makes synced local for the print too - both raise UnboundLocalError. A function that only reads synced sees the global. `failed.append(...)` does not assign to the name failed, it changes the list the name points to, so it works. With `global synced`, the assignment goes to the module variable - it works, but hidden module state like this is a smell.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "a() - only reads synced",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "b() - synced += 1",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "c() - print(synced), then synced = 5",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "d() - failed.append(\"LM-1001\")",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "e() - global synced, then synced += 1",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
